<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Sekigahara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1600">1600</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sekigahara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Sekigahara was fought in 1600 in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> between the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and a coalition of rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> house. Ieyasu's victory made him the dominant power in Japan. It led to his appointment as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> in 1603 and the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara.jpg" alt="Battle of Sekigahara" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User LordAmeth on en.wikipediaCollection of The Town of Sekigahara Archive of History and Cultural Anthropology · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sekigaharascreen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in his Kanto domains around Edo while avoiding premature ambition, before defeating the western coalition led by Ishida Mitsunari in the name of Hideyoshi's young heir at the <b>Battle of Sekigahara</b> in October 1600, one of the largest battles ever fought on Japanese soil, with perhaps 160,000 combatants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>About 100 tozama daimyō, descendants of those who had fought the Tokugawa at the <b>Battle of Sekigahara</b>, held just under 38% of the land, many of them in western Japan.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The shogunate graded them by loyalty: the shinpan were Tokugawa kin, the fudai were old vassals of the house who staffed the shogun’s government, and the tozama, the “outside” lords, had submitted only after the <b>Battle of Sekigahara</b> in 1600 and were kept out of office.</blockquote><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Social Hierarchy of Tokugawa Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>The castle fell, but its resistance bought Ieyasu time to regroup and eventually win the <b>Battle of Sekigahara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Tokugawa Ieyasu defeated a coalition of western lords at <b>Sekigahara</b>, helped by defections mid-battle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-21">21 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mori led the losing side at <b>Sekigahara</b> in 1600, Tokugawa Ieyasu reduced their lands to the provinces of Suo and Nagato.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
